  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1120 PM CST Sat Nov 21 2026\n\nMidnight satellite imagery depicted the vigorous shortwave\nmoving quickly east across the eastern Texas panhandle.\nAssociated cold front had cleared the Oklahoma border by\n1030 pm, with moderate cold air advection on moderate north\nwinds in place. Radar indicated scattered showers across the\nsoutheast zones, which will end quickly by midnight as\nsubsidence arrives behind the departing shortwave. Cold\nadvection alone on north winds of 10-15 mph will allow for a \nfreeze in the 28-32 range for much of SW KS Sunday morning. \n\nCold surface high pressure will build south into Kansas Sunday,\nproviding full sunshine or nearly so, along with diminishing\nnorth winds. Still, with 850 mb temperatures reduced to near \n0C, afternoon temperatures will struggle through the 40s,\nactually several degrees below normal for late November. \n\nContinued pressure rises to near 1030 mb and surface winds\ntrending light and variable will create near ideal radiational\ncooling Sunday night and Monday morning. Opted to undercut \nNBM minimum temperature guidance, closer to the 00z MAV which\nforecasts many locations to reach the lower 20s for several\nhours predawn Monday. A widespread killing freeze remains on\ntrack, one of the seasonally latest on record for much of \nSW KS. \n\nNext shortwave over the Great Basin 6 am Monday will be entering \nSW KS 6 am Tuesday. This system has never appeared impressive on\nthe model guidance, presenting as a progressive moisture-starved\nshortwave with little fanfare - and this thinking remains the\nsame with high confidence. Increasing clouds are expected\nMonday as the shortwave approaches, but Monday will remain\ndry. Models show little to no warming of the lower troposphere\nMonday, with 850 mb temperatures remaining near 0C, as the cool\ncontinental polar air is recirculated on southeast winds. NBM \ncorrectly keeps Monday temperatures below normal, restricted to\nthe lower to mid 40s. \n\nModels show continuity showing the most favorable - although\nstill weak - forcing for ascent focusing on the western zones\n(west of US 83) during the predawn hours Tuesday morning. Global\nmodels agree flurries or a light dusting of snow is possible\nadjacent to the Colorado border during this brief window of\nlift. Subsidence quickly reclaims control behind the exiting\nshortwave during the day Tuesday with clouds decreasing by \nafternoon. Cold air will be reinforced by the passing shortwave,\nwith 850 mb temperatures held below 0C. As such, most of \nTuesday will be spent in the 30s. \n\nDry weather is expected for the remainder of Thanksgiving week\nthrough next weekend, providing excellent holiday travel weather\nwith no impacts. Afternoon temperatures will gradually moderate\nwith time, with just occasional dry cold fronts/wind shifts amid\nquiet dry NWly midlevel flow.",
